{title: Beneath the Cross of Jesus}
{artist: Elizabeth Clephane & Frederick Maker}
{key: Eb}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 72}

Verse 1
Be[Eb]neath the cross of [Bb]Jesus I [Ab]fain would take my [Bb]stand —
the [Eb]shadow of a [Cm]mighty rock with[F]in a weary [Bb]land,
a [Eb]home within the [Bb]wilderness, a [Ab]rest upon the [Bb]way,
from the [Eb]burning of the [Ab]noontide heat and the [Bb]burden of the [Eb]day.

Verse 2
O [Eb]safe and happy [Bb]shelter, O [Ab]refuge tried and [Bb]sweet,
O [Eb]trysting place where [Cm]Heaven's love [F]and Heaven's justice [Bb]meet!
As [Eb]to the holy [Bb]patriarch that [Ab]wondrous dream was [Bb]giv'n,
So seems [Eb]my Savior's cross [Ab]to me, a [Bb]ladder up to [Eb]heav'n.

Verse 3
There [Eb]lies beneath its [Bb]shadow but [Ab]on the further [Bb]side
The [Eb]darkness of an [Cm]awful grave that gapes [F]both deep and [Bb]wide
And [Eb]there between us stands [Bb]the cross [Ab]two arms outstretched to [Bb]save
A watchman [Eb]set to [Ab]guard the way from [Bb]that eternal [Eb]grave.

Verse 4
Upon [Eb]that cross of Jesus [Bb]mine [Ab]eye at times can [Bb]see
The [Eb]very dying form [Cm]of One Who [F]suffered there for [Bb]me;
And [Eb]from my stricken [Bb]heart with tears [Ab]two wonders I [Bb]confess;
The [Eb]wonders of [Ab]redeeming love and [Bb]my [Eb]unworthiness.

Verse 5
I take, O cross, [Bb]thy shadow [Ab]for my abiding place; [Bb]I ask
no [Eb]other sunshine than [Cm]the sunshine of His face; [Bb]Content
to [Eb]let the world [Bb]go by [Ab]to know no [Bb]gain
or loss, [Eb]My sinful self [Ab]my only shame, my [Bb]glory all the [Eb]cross.
